Function-space isomorphisms for products, infinite sequences, and sums, including distribution of products over sums.

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`funProdEquiv`).** Maps into a product are order-isomorphic to pairs of maps into the factors.

*Proof.* Pair and project via the pairing–projection laws; order equivalence is componentwise. ∎

**Definition (`funProdIso`).** Function-space elements into a product are order-isomorphic to the product of the function spaces.

*Proof.* Transport the previous equivalence through the function-space element isomorphisms and the product-element isomorphism. ∎

**Theorem (`funProd_isomorphic`).** The function space into a product is isomorphic to the product of the function spaces.

*Proof.* Invoke the corresponding element order isomorphism. ∎

**Theorem (`exists_forall_swap`).** A sequence neighbourhood belongs fibrewise to the values of a family of maps at an argument if and only if the assembled sequence of values belongs to that neighbourhood.

*Proof.* Compare fibre membership with membership of indexed pairs. ∎

**Theorem (`le_of_projComp_le`).** Pointwise approximation of projections after maps into the sequence system yields approximation of the maps.

*Proof.* Compare via the sequence reconstruction from components. ∎

**Definition (`mapOfSeq`).** A sequence of maps into a base determines a map into the sequence system by requiring fibrewise membership of the values.

*Proof.* Filter axioms follow fibrewise from the terms. ∎

**Definition (`piCongrOrderIso`).** A family of order isomorphisms induces an order isomorphism of dependent products.

*Proof.* Apply componentwise; order equivalence is componentwise. ∎

**Theorem (`projComp_mono`).** Approximation of maps into the sequence system yields approximation after each projection.

*Proof.* Apply monotonicity of the projection. ∎

**Theorem (`toElementMap_mapOfSeq`).** Elementwise, a sequence of maps sends an argument to the sequence of its values.

*Proof.* Compare membership via fibres. ∎

**Definition (`funIterEquiv`).** Maps into the sequence system are order-isomorphic to sequences of maps into the base.

*Proof.* The round trips are the sequence of projections and the map of a sequence; order equivalence uses the projection comparison. ∎

**Definition (`funIterIso`).** Function-space elements into the sequence system are order-isomorphic to the sequence system on the function space.

*Proof.* Transport the previous equivalence through the function-space and sequence-element isomorphisms. ∎

**Theorem (`funIter_isomorphic`).** The function space into the sequence system is isomorphic to the sequence system on the function space.

*Proof.* Invoke the corresponding element order isomorphism. ∎

**Definition (`copair`).** The copairing of two maps into a common codomain relates a sum neighbourhood by routing left and right injections through the corresponding maps.

*Proof.* Domain and codomain membership, master, intersection on the right, and monotonicity follow by cases on sum neighbourhoods. ∎

**Definition (`copairProj`).** The pair of projections of a map out of a sum is the pair of that map after the two injections.

*Proof.* By definition. ∎

**Theorem (`prod_mem_nonempty`).** Under nonempty-neighbourhood hypotheses on the factors, every product neighbourhood is nonempty.

*Proof.* Pick witnesses in each projected neighbourhood. ∎

**Definition (`distribMap`).** Distribution of a product over a sum pairs the first projection with the left injection after the second projection.

*Proof.* Assemble pairing and composition of the product projections with the left injection. ∎

**Theorem (`copair_comp_inMap₀`).** The copair after the left injection recovers the left summand map.

*Proof.* Compare relations via left injections. ∎

**Theorem (`copair_comp_inMap₁`).** The copair after the right injection recovers the right summand map.

*Proof.* Compare relations via right injections. ∎

**Theorem (`copairProj_copair`).** Projecting a copair recovers the original pair of maps.

*Proof.* Apply the two injection laws. ∎
